      "title": "Evidence-guided compilation for context-dependent optimization",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Shared concept",
          "body": "The sources present compiler optimization as a decision process improved by evidence about program behavior, program representation, hardware context, or execution constraints rather than by fixed local rules alone. Profile-guided C optimization uses run-time execution information to focus classic transformations on hot regions. Machine-learning compilation trains empirical models from program features and performance labels. Distributed quantum compilation must account for network timing, Bell-state availability, communication latency, and communication-qubit limits when mapping algorithms to distributed hardware."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Profile, learned, and architectural evidence",
          "body": "Weighted flow graphs and execution profiles expose frequently executed classical paths; feature vectors make source, intermediate-representation, and dynamic properties learnable; distributed quantum coupling maps encode remote-operation constraints that change over time. These evidence sources differ in form, but each gives the compiler a basis for deciding where transformations or mappings are likely to pay off."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Synthesis value",
          "body": "A synthesis page can compare profiling counts, learned program features, and distributed coupling-map dynamics as parallel answers to the same compiler-design question: what evidence should guide optimization decisions when costs depend on context?"
        }
