      "topic_id": "topic-representations-for-optimization-scope",
      "title": "Representations that define optimization scope",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Shared concept",
          "body": "Across the sources, the representation chosen by the compiler determines which optimization opportunities, dependencies, and constraints become visible. Profile-guided classic optimization turns hot traces into super-blocks. Machine-learning optimization depends on feature representations and dimension reduction. Distributed quantum compilation represents a networked quantum machine through processors, communication qubits, quantum links, topology constraints, and remote operations."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Representation choices",
          "body": "Super-blocks are linear basic-block sequences with a single entry and multiple exits, formed by trace selection and tail duplication to expose hot-path transformations. Compiler-learning systems use quantifiable program features, but high-dimensional feature spaces often require pruning or projection before models can generalize. Distributed quantum compilers model worst-case and topology-dependent connectivity so remote CNOTs, entanglement swapping, and data-qubit placement can be reasoned about explicitly."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Synthesis value",
          "body": "A synthesis page can treat super-blocks, feature vectors, and quantum coupling maps as examples of compiler representations that are not neutral: each representation decides what the optimizer can see, what costs it can estimate, and what transformations or mappings it can legally consider."
        }
